{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Restrict deploy job to beta tag filters",
    "Explanation": [
      "The workflow report shows every job, including `deploy`, completed with SUCCESS, so the pipeline is green. However, the trigger was an API run on the branch `koans-09-tag-filters` (commit de37612) — a non-tagged branch commit — and `deploy` still ran. That violates the requirement that `deploy` only run for tags matching the beta product (e.g. `beta-main-0.1.1`) and never for `alpha-` tags or plain branch commits. The cause is that config_7.yml has no `filters` on any workflow job; `requires` only controls ordering, not whether a job is eligible to run, and by default CircleCI runs jobs on all branches and on no tags. The fix is to add `filters` to the `deploy` job in the workflow: `tags: only: /^beta-.*/` and `branches: ignore: /.*/`, so it runs only on beta-prefixed tags and is skipped for alpha tags and branch pushes. Note that because `deploy` requires `test` which requires `build`, those upstream jobs must also be given `filters: tags: only: /.*/` so they execute on tag pipelines; otherwise `deploy` would never be able to run on a beta tag. Filters belong in the workflow job invocation, not on the job definition."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"behaviour_correct\": false,",
      "  \"fix\": \"filters_tags_only_beta\",",
      "  \"fix_target_job\": \"deploy\",",
      "  \"pipeline_green\": true",
      "}"
    ],
    "ExpectedAnswer": [
      [
        "{",
        "  \"behaviour_correct\": false,",
        "  \"fix\": \"filters_tags_only_beta\",",
        "  \"fix_target_job\": \"all_jobs\",",
        "  \"pipeline_green\": true",
        "}"
      ]
    ],
